      definition: `Baseline sexual desire: how often and how strongly thoughts, fantasies and urges toward sexual activity arise for this character even when no particular partner or situation is given, and therefore how often it actually wants sex. Anchor it on the sexual desire and sex drive measures (the frequency of sexual thoughts and fantasies, of wanted sex, of masturbation). High: sexual thoughts are frequent without a stimulus and, in a couple, the character is the one who wants more and often starts. Low: spontaneous urges are rare and desire mostly follows after an intimate mood or arousal that came first (responsive desire). Read the trait from general desire and solitary desire (wanting sex alone). Desire toward one particular partner is the most state-like part of all, swayed by the relationship, anger and fatigue, so leave it as a scene state; fantasies about people outside a committed relationship drop a lot while in a relationship, so do not use them as a marker of baseline desire. Interest decides only how often the character wants. It does not decide what must be in place before sex (sexualSpontaneity: behavior and desire overlap only weakly, and a relationship lowers desire while barely moving the threshold), whether sex is held acceptable (sexualOpenness: religiosity and guilt reach the attitude and lower desire only a little), whether an act continues once started (sexualMomentum: excitation and inhibition are different variables, so desire can be middling with easy arousal and weak inhibition) or the pull toward one person (a relation value). High interest with low openness is allowed.`,
